    // assertFunctionNames enumerates this repo's real custom assertion-delegate helpers (each
    // one is the sole statement in at least one test body) rather than a blanket wildcard, so a
    // genuinely assertion-less future test named `assertFoo`/`expectFoo` still gets caught. The
    // one exception is "**.expect": oxlint's default assertFunctionNames only recognizes a bare
    // `expect` call, not a `.expect(...)` member call ending an arbitrary-length chain, so every
    // supertest `request(app).get(url).expect(status)` assertion in this repo was invisible to
    // this rule (see #10657) until this pattern was added. Keep in sync with the identical
    // vitest/expect-expect list below. backend/agents/.oxlintrc.json, backend/scripts/.oxlintrc.json,
    // backend/services/crawler-rss/.oxlintrc.json, and backend/test-helpers/entities/.oxlintrc.json
    // each have their own .oxlintrc.json and previously set this rule to a local "off" (the actual
    // #10657 blindspot); removing that local override is enough for them to inherit this array via
    // "extends" -- verified empirically that a directory's own .oxlintrc.json only loses an
    // ancestor's "overrides" entries, not its base "rules", so no restatement is needed there.
    "jest/expect-expect": [
